# Settings of the standalone server (docker-compose.yml in this folder).
#
# ./init-env.sh <domain> copies this file to .env and fills in the domain and
# fresh secrets. Without the script, copy it by hand and generate each secret
# with `openssl rand -hex 32`. Never commit .env.

# === Address ===

# The domain name of the installation, for example rallye.example.org. Its DNS
# record must point at this server before the first start.
CLAVIS_DOMAIN=

# === Secrets ===

# Password of the PostgreSQL user clavis. Hex characters only, because it
# becomes part of the database URL.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# Signs the session tokens. At least 32 characters.
JWT_SECRET_KEY=
# Chains the audit log. Keep it as long as the audit log exists.
HMAC_AUDIT_KEY=
# Internal secret of the object store, 64 hex characters.
GARAGE_RPC_SECRET=
# Key pair of the object store. The access key is GK plus 24 hex characters,
# the secret key 64 hex characters. The store creates the key at its first start.
S3_ACCESS_KEY=
S3_SECRET_KEY=

# === Address search ===

# none: no search text leaves the server. Import the place index of your region
# once, see "First steps" in deployment/README.md. Other values: nominatim,
# photon. Those send the search text to an outside service.
GEOCODER_UPSTREAM=none
# Mail address or URL of the operator. The public services ask for it.
# GEOCODER_CONTACT=you@example.org

# === Mail (optional) ===
#
# Without SMTP_HOST the app writes each mail to its log instead of sending it.
# Invitations and password resets then reach nobody, so set this up before you
# invite people.
# SMTP_HOST=mail.example.org
# SMTP_PORT=465
# ssl (port 465), starttls (port 587) or none
# SMTP_TLS_MODE=ssl
# SMTP_USERNAME=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# An address your mail server accepts as sender, and a mailbox a person reads.
# MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=noreply@example.org
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=team@example.org
# MAIL_FROM_NAME=Clavis

# === Product name ===

# Shown in the app shell, the page title and the mail signature.
# BRAND_NAME=Clavis

# === Behind a reverse proxy of your own ===
#
# When another proxy on this server or in front of it terminates TLS, Caddy
# serves plain HTTP. Point your proxy at the HTTP port and set:
# CLAVIS_SITE=:80
# CLAVIS_HTTP_PORT=127.0.0.1:8080
# CLAVIS_TRUSTED_PROXIES=private_ranges
# Publish the HTTPS port somewhere unused, because Caddy does not use it then:
# CLAVIS_HTTPS_PORT=127.0.0.1:8443
# The public origin, when it is not https://CLAVIS_DOMAIN:
# CLAVIS_ORIGIN=https://rallye.example.org

# Any other setting of the API may go into this file as well, for example the
# deletion periods LK_A_DAYS, LK_B_DAYS, LK_C_DAYS and LK_H_DAYS, or
# LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT. The full list with explanations is ../.env.example.
